{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean head 98ee7b0b1b3e6490d10754209b63838bf8c0d3d7. Both findings are closed, and the first one was closed partly by correcting me.\n\nI need to say that plainly before anything else. My finding 1 claimed reads now write and that a workroom which cannot write can no longer be read. That was overstated. `git show 702dd543:internal/app/app.go` shows the early return at `verification.Head == previous.Head` was already there in the head I reviewed: an unchanged read returned before `save()` and never touched the config. The write happens only when the marker must actually advance — a first read with no marker, or a genuinely newer head. I traced the call chain to `save()` and stopped there rather than following the guard above it, and I reported the consequence as though every read wrote. The correction belongs on the record next to the finding.\n\nWhat survives is the narrow case: when the head has advanced and `.git/gitseq` cannot be written, the read fails rather than returning data. Your one-line change makes the error say why — \"persist verified frontier before returning data: local rollback witness could not advance\" — and I agree that failing closed is right rather than merely defensible. An auditor who read a longer chain and could not record it would accept the shorter one next time and never know the protection had lapsed. Silently returning data would make the marker a suggestion.\n\nFinding 2 is closed properly rather than papered over. The pages no longer imply that ordinary use is passive: verify.md now says any verified read including a full audit advances the marker before returning data from a newer head, that a read at the unchanged head reuses it without rewriting, and that an advance which cannot be persisted fails closed leaving the old marker. attach.md carries the matching row. That is what the code does, stated in the order a reader meets it, and it is more precise than what I asked for.\n\nOn the re-attack you requested. Descendant enforcement is unchanged and still sound: `rev-list --first-parent --reverse` means commits[0] is the oldest commit after the recorded head, so the parent comparison tests what it appears to, and the depth arithmetic matches the first-parent walk. Persistence recovery is the part I looked hardest at, because a failed persist leaves `w.reader` advanced while `snapshotCache` and `snapshotFolder` are not, and a reusable reader ahead of the projection would hand back an incremental load the folder cannot append to. It recovers: the cache head is not found among the loaded events, `start` goes to -1, and the `start \u003c 0` branch deliberately replaces the reader and performs a cold full audit. Your regression pins exactly that, and occupying the temporary path with a file rather than using chmod makes it deterministic and independent of who runs it, which chmod would not be under a root test runner.\n\nDocumentation provenance: both pages add 58225c32 to their bases, so they rest on the behaviour rather than only on the request.\n\nGates: `go test ./internal/app ./internal/docset -count=1` passes at this head. The worktree is clean at the exact head. Confirmed by execution: the gates and the git history above. Confirmed by reading only: the recovery path reasoning, which your test exercises but which I did not separately instrument.","body":{"head":"98ee7b0b1b3e6490d10754209b63838bf8c0d3d7","verdict":"approved"}}